WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:13.620
Many C++ systems contain raw owning pointers, manual delete paths, pointer caches, mixed allocation conventions, and comments that describe lifetime rules no one fully trusts anymore.

2
00:00:13.720 --> 00:00:18.520
Smart pointer migration should begin with ownership mapping, not search-and-replace.

3
00:00:18.620 --> 00:00:26.380
Identify who creates each object, who owns it, who merely observes it, who may store access, and who destroys it.

4
00:00:26.660 --> 00:00:30.340
Replace owning raw pointers before observer pointers.

5
00:00:30.440 --> 00:00:37.000
If one component clearly owns the object, std::unique_ptr is often the safest migration target.

6
00:00:37.100 --> 00:00:40.620
It reduces manual cleanup and makes transfer explicit.

7
00:00:40.720 --> 00:00:49.680
Use std::shared_ptr only when the existing design truly requires shared lifetime, and record why sharing is necessary.

8
00:00:49.780 --> 00:00:55.860
Otherwise, migration can turn unclear raw ownership into unclear shared ownership.

9
00:00:56.140 --> 00:00:59.420
Refactoring should be incremental and test-driven.

10
00:00:59.520 --> 00:01:09.200
Add regression tests around destruction order, error paths, early returns, cancellation, and object graph behavior before making broad changes.

11
00:01:09.300 --> 00:01:14.420
Watch for APIs that return raw pointers, store callbacks, or expose caches.

12
00:01:14.520 --> 00:01:18.040
These are places where ownership and observation can become tangled.

13
00:01:18.320 --> 00:01:24.800
Some raw pointers may remain as borrowed access, especially at legacy or C interfaces.

14
00:01:24.900 --> 00:01:27.460
Document those conventions and keep them narrow.

15
00:01:27.560 --> 00:01:38.280
The goal is not to eliminate every raw pointer; it is to eliminate hidden ownership and make remaining borrowing rules clear enough for maintainers, reviewers, and tools to reason about.
